@name         rm
@version      1.0.0
@category     coreutil
@wave         R50
@repo         github.com/paideia-os/rm
@license      MIT

@synopsis
  rm [-r|-f|-v|--wipe|--dry-run] <path>...

@description
  Remove one or more filesystem entries. Every entry is journaled to a
  per-user trash subtree inside a PdxFS v1 transaction before it stops
  being reachable by name, so a removal within the retention window
  (default 24 hours) is fully reversible by `undo` — the invariant I5
  (design/tooling/plan.md §4.2) rm exists to demonstrate.

  --wipe overrides the journal path: the trash entry is unlinked at
  once and the underlying storage is byte-overwritten where the device
  supports it. A --wipe removal is intentionally not reversible; a
  positive was_wiped audit flag lets a forensic reader distinguish an
  intentional shred from a later retention reap.

@options
  -r, --recursive
       Descend into directories leaf-first inside the same TXN.
       Every entry is trash-moved individually so `undo` can partial-
       replay by walking the schema stream in reverse.

  -f, --force
       Skip per-file confirmation. At v1.0 rm has no stdin binding and
       every non-`-f` removal is treated as `-f` after emitting a
       stdout note under -v; the interactive prompt lands with the
       shell.M3 stdin plumbing wave.

  -v, --verbose
       Emit a per-target line naming the observable action (would
       remove … / rm: … / recursive walk of … / shred: …) and every
       decision the removal took (retention deadline, confirmation
       skip, elevate refusal).

  --wipe
       Forensic shred (see @description). Skips the trash-subtree
       journal, skips the undo record, stamps was_wiped=1 in the
       RemoveRecord.

  --dry-run
       Emit what would happen and return EXIT_OK. Zero side effects
       on the filesystem, on the audit stream, on the schema stream,
       and on the undo log. The dry-run path is not a preview of a
       forensic-detection-safe shred; --wipe --dry-run declines to
       emit a wipe stub and prints the ordinary dry-run line.

@differences-from-posix
  * `rm` never prompts on a read-only file. Cap-check for
    KIND_PDXFS_FILE(write,<target-parent>) is the sole authority.

  * `rm -rf /` never reaches a cap-check: `-rf` is a clustered short
    flag and libpdx-argv rejects clustering outright (`ERR_CLUSTERED_
    SHORT`), exiting 2 before rm's body ever runs. Typed as `rm -r -f
    /`, the target IS distinguished — not by a heuristic hard-code of
    "/", "/*", or a similar list, but because the loader's cap_
    manifest_verify (libpdx-cap M2-001) denies the write at exec time
    if the root subtree is outside rm's narrowed KIND_PDXFS_FILE
    scope; rm's own body never observes or returns that denial.

  * Every removal is undoable within the retention window. POSIX rm
    is destructive-on-return; Paideia rm returns before the trash
    reaper has run and `undo` can reverse the last N invocations
    (design/tooling/plan.md §I5).

  * `--wipe` is the forensic path and is explicit. POSIX has no
    portable shred; the closest ancestor is GNU shred(1) as a
    separate binary. rm --wipe subsumes it under the same TXN
    discipline.

  * Every action goes through libpdx-audit before the trash-move.
    A downstream reader of the RemoveRecord[] stream sees every
    removal in emission order, not filesystem order.

  * Targets under `/system/` (or crossing the invoker's subtree)
    take a libpdx-elevate round-trip before proceeding. A refused
    elevate skips the removal — no confirm hop, no print, no
    retention or undo emit; the operator sees the refusal via the
    rm_blocked_by_elevate counter surface.

@exit-codes
  0  Success (every target processed).
  1  At least one target failed against the substrate.
  2  Usage error (bad flag, missing positional; also a clustered
     short flag such as `-rf`, which libpdx-argv rejects outright).
  3  EXIT_NOT_YET_IMPL — reserved for a flag combination an M2+ body
     recognises but does not yet implement. Not returned by any path
     at this release.
  4  Capability denied. Raised by the loader's cap_manifest_verify at
     exec time (target-parent write cap missing, or a --no-cap:…
     stripped the cap the request needs) — a cap-denied process never
     starts, so rm's own body never observes or returns this code.
  5  Signature verification failure (raised by pkg during install,
     never by rm at run-time).

@see
  undo(1)   — reverse the last N rm invocations within the window.
  cp(1)     — the mirror-image write path (also I5-journaled).
  mv(1)     — cross-subtree rename (rm + cp under one TXN).
  caps(1)   — inspect the caps rm requests via caps.decl.
  pkg(1)    — install / upgrade rm via `pkg install rm`.
  doc(1)    — read this file: `doc rm`.

@examples
  # Remove one file, verbose:
  $ rm -v report.txt
  rm: report.txt
  rm: trash retention: 24h

  # Remove a directory recursively:
  $ rm -r stale/
  rm: recursive walk of: stale
  rm: 12 entries trash-moved (undo window: 24h)

  # Preview a removal:
  $ rm --dry-run -v secrets.txt
  rm: would remove: secrets.txt

  # Forensic shred:
  $ rm --wipe -v scratch.key
  rm: shred: scratch.key

  # Reverse the last remove within the window:
  $ undo
  undo: rm -v report.txt  →  restored (deadline had 23h51m left)

@ergonomics
  Five most-common tasks, each ≤ 3 keystrokes past the target:
    1. remove a file           →  rm  <path>
    2. remove a tree           →  rm -r <path>
    3. preview a removal       →  rm --dry-run <path>
    4. shred (no undo)         →  rm --wipe <path>
    5. undo the last removal   →  undo   (shipped by undo(1); rm's
                                  RemoveRecord + undo record combine
                                  to make this a one-word recovery.)

@since 1.0.0
  Initial signed release. Milestones M1-M5 landed 2026-08 across
  issues #1-#17; see CHANGELOG.md for the per-milestone breakdown.
